#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"</AccordionItem>\n"
"</Accordion>\n"
"This page demonstrates how to use the [`PadDynamicalDecoupling`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.transpiler.passes.PadDynamicalDecoupling) pass to add an error suppression technique called _dynamical decoupling_ to the circuit.\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Dynamical decoupling works by adding pulse sequences (known as _dynamical decoupling sequences_) to idle qubits to flip them around the Bloch sphere, which cancels the effect of noise channels, thereby suppressing decoherence. These pulse sequences are similar to refocusing pulses used in nuclear magnetic resonance. For a full description, see [A Quantum Engineer's Guide to Superconducting Qubits](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06560).\n"
msgstr "Gumagana ang dynamical decoupling sa pamamagitan ng pagdaragdag ng mga pulse sequence (kilala bilang _dynamical decoupling sequences_) sa mga idle qubit para i-flip ang mga ito sa paligid ng Bloch sphere, na nagkakansela sa epekto ng mga noise channel, sa gayon ay sinusupress ang decoherence. Ang mga pulse sequence na ito ay katulad ng mga refocusing pulse na ginagamit sa nuclear magnetic resonance. Para sa kumpletong paglalarawan, tingnan ang [A Quantum Engineer's Guide to Superconducting Qubits](https://arxiv.org/abs/1904.06560).\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Because the `PadDynamicalDecoupling` pass only operates on scheduled circuits and involves gates that are not necessarily basis gates of our target, you will need the [`ALAPScheduleAnalysis`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.transpiler.passes.ALAPScheduleAnalysis) and [`BasisTranslator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.transpiler.passes.BasisTranslator) passes as well.\n"
msgstr "Dahil ang `PadDynamicalDecoupling` pass ay gumagana lamang sa mga naka-schedule na circuit at kasangkot ang mga gate na hindi kinakailangang basis gate ng ating target, kakailanganin mo rin ang mga [`ALAPScheduleAnalysis`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.transpiler.passes.ALAPScheduleAnalysis) at [`BasisTranslator`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.transpiler.passes.BasisTranslator) pass.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Get the `target` information from the `backend` and save the operation names as `basis_gates` because the `target` will need to be modified to add timing information for the gates used in dynamical decoupling.\n"
msgstr "Kunin ang `target` information mula sa `backend` at i-save ang mga pangalan ng operation bilang `basis_gates` dahil kakailanganing i-modify ang `target` para magdagdag ng timing information para sa mga gate na ginagamit sa dynamical decoupling.\n"

# doq-bootstrap: dropped, JSX/HTML tags differ from English: Kunin ang
# impormasyon ng `target` mula sa `backend` at i-sav
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid ""
"<Admonition type=\"note\">\n"
"The  `FakeSherbrooke` mock backend from `qiskit_ibm_runtime` is used in these examples, but you can try it on any Qiskit-compatible real or fake backend.  Your results might be different.\n"
"</Admonition>\n"
msgstr ""

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Create an `efficient_su2` circuit as an example. First, transpile the circuit to the backend because dynamical decoupling pulses need to be added after the circuit has been transpiled and scheduled. Dynamical decoupling often works best when there is a lot of idle time in the quantum circuits - that is, there are qubits that are not being used while others are active. This is the case in this circuit because the two-qubit `ecr` gates are applied sequentially in this ansatz.\n"
msgstr "Gumawa ng `efficient_su2` circuit bilang halimbawa. Una, i-transpile ang circuit papunta sa backend dahil kailangang idagdag ang mga dynamical decoupling pulse pagkatapos na ma-transpile at ma-schedule ang circuit. Ang dynamical decoupling ay kadalasang gumagana nang pinakamahusay kapag may maraming idle time sa mga quantum circuit - ibig sabihin, may mga qubit na hindi ginagamit habang aktibo ang iba. Ito ang kaso sa circuit na ito dahil ang mga two-qubit `ecr` gate ay inilalapat nang sunud-sunod sa ansatz na ito.\n"

# doq: copied, nothing translatable
#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/guides/dynamical-decoupling-pass-manager/extracted-outputs/9479a60c-d5d0-45c7-a93e-a2a488ba8985-0.svg)\n"
msgstr "![Output of the previous code cell](/docs/images/guides/dynamical-decoupling-pass-manager/extracted-outputs/9479a60c-d5d0-45c7-a93e-a2a488ba8985-0.svg)\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "A dynamical decoupling sequence is a series of gates that compose to the identity and are spaced regularly in time. For example, start by creating a simple sequence called XY4 consisting of four gates.\n"
msgstr "Ang isang dynamical decoupling sequence ay isang serye ng mga gate na kung pagsasamahin ay bumubuo sa identity at pantay-pantay ang agwat sa oras. Bilang halimbawa, magsimula sa paglikha ng isang simpleng sequence na tinatawag na XY4 na binubuo ng apat na gate.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Because of the regular timing of dynamical decoupling sequences, information about the `YGate` must be added to the `target` because it is *not* a basis gate, whereas the `XGate` is. We know *a priori* that the `YGate` has the same duration and error as the `XGate`, however, so we can just retrieve those properties from the `target` and add them back for the `YGate` objects. This is also why the `basis_gates` were saved separately, since we are adding the `YGate` instruction to the `target` although it is not an actual basis gate of `ibm_fez`.\n"
msgstr "Dahil sa regular na timing ng mga dynamical decoupling sequence, kailangang idagdag ang impormasyon tungkol sa `YGate` sa `target` dahil ito ay *hindi* basis gate, samantalang basis gate ang `XGate`. Alam natin *a priori* na ang `YGate` ay may parehong duration at error gaya ng `XGate`, kaya maaari lamang nating kunin ang mga property na iyon mula sa `target` at idagdag muli ang mga ito para sa mga `YGate` object. Ito rin ang dahilan kung bakit hiwalay na na-save ang `basis_gates`, dahil idinadagdag natin ang `YGate` instruction sa `target` kahit na hindi ito talagang basis gate ng `ibm_fez`.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Ansatz circuits such as `efficient_su2` are parameterized, so they must have value bound to them before being sent to the backend. Here, assign random parameters.\n"
msgstr "Ang mga ansatz circuit tulad ng `efficient_su2` ay parameterized, kaya kailangang may value na naka-bind sa mga ito bago ipadala sa backend. Dito, magtalaga ng mga random na parameter.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Next, execute the custom passes. Instantiate the `PassManager` with `ALAPScheduleAnalysis` and `PadDynamicalDecoupling`.  Run `ALAPScheduleAnalysis` first to add timing information about the quantum circuit before the regularly-spaced dynamical decoupling sequences can be added. These passes are run on the circuit with `.run()`.\n"
msgstr "Sunod, isagawa ang mga custom pass. I-instantiate ang `PassManager` gamit ang `ALAPScheduleAnalysis` at `PadDynamicalDecoupling`. Patakbuhin muna ang `ALAPScheduleAnalysis` para magdagdag ng timing information tungkol sa quantum circuit bago maidagdag ang mga pantay-pantay na dynamical decoupling sequence. Ang mga pass na ito ay pinapatakbo sa circuit gamit ang `.run()`.\n"

#. type: Plain text
#, no-wrap
msgid "Use the visualization tool [`timeline_drawer`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.visualization.timeline_drawer) to see the circuit's timing and confirm that a regularly-spaced sequence of `XGate` objects and `YGate` objects appear in the circuit.\n"
msgstr "Gamitin ang visualization tool na [`timeline_drawer`](https://docs.quantum.ibm.com/api/qiskit/qiskit.visualization.timeline_drawer) para makita ang timing ng circuit at kumpirmahin na lumalabas sa circuit ang isang pantay-pantay na sequence ng mga `XGate` object at `YGate` object.\n"

